															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2203795&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608196096" alt="Kalinin" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2203795)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/kalinin" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2203795); return false;" >Kalinin</a></span> <span class="odate time_1474995264 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">27 Sep 2016 16:54</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2661675">
									

<p>Neutral vote. It's competently written, and a nice little piece of horror well within the site's traditional view of such things. Indeed, it's a full realization of something close to what I imagine to be the majority view of how D-Class work in the Foundation. That's well within the spirit of both the site and the contest, and thus I'll refrain from letting personal stuff affect my vote.</p>
<p>That said, while it is the prevailing view of D-Class, it's one that I have a lot of issues with from a suspension of disbelief perspective. Human experimentation is played for a lot of horror on the site, both here and in many, many other works. But I've never really found a convincing narrative about how educated people who are presumably not the products of radicalized political systems as a general rule are totally fine with things that would have gotten them hung at Nuremburg or Tokyo.</p>
<p>There are nods to that problem, and I appreciate the attempt to deal with those ramifications in this tale, but the researchers still come across as a little to blasé about what they do. I mean, if you talk to social workers, they'll tell you about burnout, as they have to be exposed to all the shitty things that people do to each other, and a <em>lot</em> of them leave the profession because of that. But then you have someone who's <em>perpetrating</em> those things, for years and years. It's a difficult line to walk, because I've read far too many tales that go in the other direction and lament the horrible evil Foundation in a goofy manner, and maybe I'm being overly picky, but I've yet to see this interpretation of D-Class personnel get correctly the fact that we're dealing with legitimate crimes against humanity, here.</p>
<p>Anyway. It was a decent piece, and I can't vote against it, but I've got a raft of personal interpretation stuff that prevents me from upvoting.</p>

							</div>

								
					
